# S12.1 r5 named-POSIX-ACL orphan recovery diagnosis

## Observed delta

The sole r5 recovery invocation stopped before journal release, backup or a
new deployment attempt with `S12_1_REPOSITORY_PARENT_RED`. The canonical
repository parent remains `chatops:chatops 2770`; both canonical repositories
retain their recorded ownership/modes and installed `.git` directories. The
r5 upload inputs and trusted-controller copy were removed by the operator
wrapper.

Read-only aggregation across the repository parent, both repository roots and
all tracked barrier paths found 882 protected paths. 702 carry named POSIX ACL
entries. Only `system.posix_acl_access` and `system.posix_acl_default` occur;
there are no read errors or other xattr-name classes. These ACLs are an
intentional runtime-access property of the canonical repository ancestry, not
repository or release drift.

## Root cause

The pristine-V1 direct-release classifier accepted only base POSIX ACL entries.
That contract cannot recover the legitimate canonical server posture even
though the path performs no ownership, mode or ACL transition and already
holds continuous parent, tracked-worktree and Git-metadata mutation guards
through durable journal release.

## r6 correction

The smallest safe correction accepts named user/group entries only inside a
structurally complete POSIX access/default ACL. Base owner, owning-group and
other entries remain unique and mandatory; named principals require a real
numeric identifier, uniqueness and exactly one mask. Unknown xattrs, malformed
ACLs, duplicate principals and unmasked named entries remain RED. The existing
continuous fingerprints bind the exact ACL bytes before the legacy journal is
released, and the direct-release path still performs no chmod, chown or ACL
rewrite.

Product and provider boundaries are unchanged: Yoti remains Sandbox-only and
real AVS, Adult media/submission, community Adult media, external publishing,
payments, Controlled Beta and Production remain closed.
